  "level": 3,
  "question": {
    "text": "A metallic block of mass $20 \\mathrm{~kg}$ is dragged with a uniform velocity of $0.5 \\mathrm{~ms}^{-1}$ on a horizontal table for $2.1 \\mathrm{~s}$. The co-efficient of static friction between the block and the table is $0.10$. What will be the maximum possible rise in temperature of the metal block if the specific heat of the block is $0.1$ C.G.S unit ? Assume $g=10 \\mathrm{~ms}^{-2}$ uniform rise in temperature throughout the whole block. [lgnore absorption of heat by the table]",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "jOymv",
      "text": "$0.0025^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$",
      "isCorrect": true,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "wZfGN",
      "text": "$0.025^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "42gVn",
      "text": "$0.001^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "U84CH",
      "text": "$0.05^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "Hint: \r\nfriction $f=\\mu \\mathrm{mg}=0.1 \\times 20 \\times 10=20$\r\n$\\mathrm{~W}_{\\mathrm{f}}=$ heat\r\n$\\Rightarrow(\\mathrm{f.u}) \\mathrm{t}=\\mathrm{mc} \\Delta \\mathrm{T}$\r\n$\\Rightarrow 20 \\times 0.5 \\times 2.1=20 \\times 0.1 \\times 4.2 \\times 10^{3} . \\Delta \\mathrm{T}$\r\n$\\Rightarrow \\Delta \\mathrm{T}=0.0025^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$",
    "image": null
  },
